ded financially if their ideas were used. For example, David", "Kahn system bar in the early 1900s consisted of a plain straight steel in the center of a molded concrete form. The beam would fail along the lines of principal stresses in the concrete through shearing. The plain straight steel was placed into an encased mold and concrete was poured in. This formulated mixture of steel and concrete then hardened and became the reinforced beam bar. But in some cases there was a tiny air gap between the steel and the hardened concrete which allowed the steel to move and slip. This weakened the reinforced beam from its original full strength capability.", "Kahn system are still permanently attached. When the wet concrete mixture was poured and hardened there was no slippage of the steel beam because the tension stress is distributed throughout the beam. This new style constructed steel beam was called \"the Kahn trussed bar\", sometimes shortened to just \"the Kahn bar\". The trussed steel bar had bent-up \"wings\" on each side. The \"wings\" on the steel bar provided 20\u201330% additional strength to a reinforced concrete beam over the old technology of plain straight smooth steel bars or rods. The primary concept of the Kahn bar was that it gave reinforcement in both"], "summary": "Sentence 1: The Kahn bar was a type of reinforced concrete beam that was designed to provide additional strength to a reinforced concrete beam over the old technology of plain straight smooth steel bars or rods."}
